  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nBroad troughing begins to take hold today which is going to \nkick off our transition toward cooler, more seasonable \ntemperatures. Northwest flow quickly drops 850 mb temperatures \ndown to +12-14C by the afternoon meaning the ceiling for surface\ntemperatures is only in the low to mid-80s south of the \nmountains, and in the mid to upper 70s to the north. CAMs are \nhinting at some isolated showers near the International Boarder \nduring the day, but with drier air also being advected in I \nimagine that is the worst case scenario. Wednesday look like a \nsimilar day temperature-wise with more zonal flow aloft, and the\ndrier airmass firmly in place. Again CAMs are trying to hint at\nisolated afternoon showers in the mountains, but they may have \ntrouble overcoming the dry air. \n\nPrecipitation chances increase late Wednesday night into \nThursday as a trough axis approaches the area. As of now models \nare suggesting the brief period of southwesterly flow leading \ninto Thursday afternoon is not going to do much to increase \nmoisture with long range ensembles and the NAM only suggesting \nPWATS climb into the 1 to 1.25 inch range (a trend downward from\nearlier). Dewpoints also look to end up in the low to mid-60s, \nand long range ensembles suggest that around 500 J/kg of CAPE \ncould form (with 25-30 kts of shear), so a strong storm is \npossible (and CSUs machine learning model has some low \nprobabilities) but it is too early to say for certain what the \nparameter space is going to look like. The other point of \nuncertainty is going to be coverage in general as the ECMWF and \nGFS ensembles have come into better agreement on the surface low\npressure forming over the St. Lawrence River and ultimately \ntracking east of Maine. This may keep coverage on the lower end,\nbut I am fine with the blanket 30-50% PoPs the NBM is \nadvertising for most of the area until we can get this event in \nthe CAM window.\n\nOne thing the aforementioned low track would certainly portend \nto is clearing out on Friday. Some showers would still be \npossible as the forcing from the trough axis moves through \nFriday afternoon, but for the most part high pressure could \nstart building in fairly quickly afterward. This may make for \nbreezy conditions Friday and Saturday as the pressure gradient \ntightens as a result, but the chance for a nice weekend is \nlooking better.",
